<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=wine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 6000 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wine is an alcoholic drink made by fermenting grapes. The earliest known traces of it, from about 6000 BCE, were found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>; jars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> Firuz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, of about 5000 BCE, held wine sealed with pine resin, and the Areni-1 cave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> holds the oldest known winery, with a press, vats and jars from about 4100 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> spread wine around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> enlarged its trade, and it became part of Jewish and Christian ritual. In the 19th century phylloxera forced the replanting of almost every vine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wine.jpg" alt="Wine" width="330" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georgeg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dionysos_Akme_Paphos_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 6000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Near Tbilisi, Georgia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Pottery jars from Jiahu in China, of the early seventh millennium BCE, may have held a fermented drink of rice and grapes, but other fruits cannot be ruled out. The Hajj Firuz jars from Iran, of about 5000 BCE, are also called the earliest firm evidence of wine production.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/pottery">Pottery<span>c. 18,000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=wine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
